Interface vs abstract class — when would you choose each?

In Java, both interfaces and abstract classes are used to achieve abstraction, but they serve different purposes.

The main difference is:
- Interface: Defines a contract — what a class must do.
- Abstract class: Provides a common base — what related classes are and what common functionality they can share.
Let's understand this with examples from your Java backend/payment project.
1. Interface
An interface defines a contract that implementing classes must follow.

Use an interface when:
- Different classes need to follow the same contract.
- Classes may be unrelated but share common behaviour.
- You want loose coupling and flexibility.
- You want to support multiple implementations.
Example: Payment Gateway

Suppose your application supports Razorpay and Stripe.
interface PaymentGateway {

    void initiatePayment(double amount);

    void refundPayment(String transactionId);
}


Implementation:
class RazorpayGateway implements PaymentGateway {

    @Override
    public void initiatePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Razorpay payment initiated");
    }

    @Override
    public void refundPayment(String transactionId) {
        System.out.println("Razorpay refund processed");
    }
}

class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway {

    @Override
    public void initiatePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Stripe payment initiated");
    }

    @Override
    public void refundPayment(String transactionId) {
        System.out.println("Stripe refund processed");
    }
}


Now the service can depend on the interface instead of a specific implementation.
class PaymentService {

    private PaymentGateway gateway;

    PaymentService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    public void pay(double amount) {
        gateway.initiatePayment(amount);
    }
}


Why interface here?

Because both gateways provide the same functionality but have different internal implementations.

We can switch implementations without changing the PaymentService business logic.

This is also an example of loose coupling and dependency injection.
2. Abstract class
An abstract class is used when multiple related classes share common properties and behaviour, but some functionality needs to be implemented differently.

Use an abstract class when:
- You want to share common code.
- You want common instance variables.
- You want constructors.
- You want to provide some implemented methods and leave others abstract.
Example: Payment Processing
abstract class Payment {

    protected double amount;
    protected String transactionId;

    public Payment(double amount, String transactionId) {
        this.amount = amount;
        this.transactionId = transactionId;
    }

    // Common functionality
    public void validatePayment() {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid amount");
        }
    }

    // Different implementations
    public abstract void processPayment();
}


Child classes:
class CardPayment extends Payment {

    public CardPayment(double amount, String id) {
        super(amount, id);
    }

    @Override
    public void processPayment() {
        System.out.println("Processing card payment");
    }
}

class UpiPayment extends Payment {

    public UpiPayment(double amount, String id) {
        super(amount, id);
    }

    @Override
    public void processPayment() {
        System.out.println("Processing UPI payment");
    }
}


Why abstract class here?

Both payment types share:
- Amount.
- Transaction ID.
- Payment validation logic.
But their payment processing logic differs.

Instead of duplicating common code, we keep it in the abstract parent class.



5. Interview-ready answer
If the interviewer asks,
 "When would you choose an interface over an abstract class?"

"I would choose an interface when I want to define a contract that multiple potentially unrelated classes can
implement. For example, in a payment application, I can define a PaymentGateway interface with initiatePayment()
and refundPayment() methods, and different gateway implementations can provide their own logic.

I would choose an abstract class when multiple related classes share common state and implementation.
For example, different payment types can extend a common Payment abstract class that contains transaction
details and common validation logic.

So, I generally prefer interfaces for loose coupling and flexibility, and abstract classes when I need to share
common state and behaviour."



